      "phase": "Assessment A: Design + Copy Review",
      "prompt": "You are Assessment A in a two-part Impeccable design critique. You are an award-winning design director. Do NOT run any detector script (detect.mjs) — that is Assessment B's job; stay isolated.\n\nRead these source files in the repo at /home/alex/Projects/ArmchairFuturistLanding and evaluate the live homepage:\n\nCompose target: src/app/page.tsx (section order + composition).\nSections (src/components/sections/): HeroSection, LogoBannerSection, WhatThisIsNotSection, MentoringSection, KeyStatsSection, ServicesSection, ROICalculatorSection, AboutMeSection, CaseStudiesSection, TestimonialsSection, SpeakingSection, InsightsSection, SubstackSection, AssessmentCtaSection, FAQSection, ConnectSection.\nLayout: src/components/layout/Header.tsx, Footer.tsx.\nAssessment flow: src/components/assessment/ (QuizQuestion, QuizProgress, EmailCapture, ResultPage, ScoreChart) + src/app/assessment/.\n\n\nBRIEF (ground truth for judgment):\n\nPRODUCT: The Armchair Futurist — \"the last AI consultant you'll ever hire.\" 1:1 AI mastery mentoring. Success = how fast the client stops paying us (self-sufficient in 8-10 weeks). Conversion surface = marketing landing page with 14 modular sections + embedded 9-question AI-readiness assessment (free, no email to see score).\nAUDIENCE: Established professionals (consultants, coaches, freelancers, agency owners) with existing clients/revenue. Time-poor, skeptical of marketing copy, abandon in <5s if it reads like every AI-consultant funnel. Already pay for AI tools, may have been burned by an \"AI workshop.\"\nBRAND VOICE (direct, warm, expert — do not break): Clarity over hype. Trust as the product. Exit as the goal. Capability not deliverable.\nANTI-SLOP KILL LIST (copy must not use): delve, landscape, tapestry, testament, navigate, it's worth noting, first and foremost, in order to, at the end of the day, journey (as business metaphor), \"unlock AI's potential\", \"navigate the AI landscape\", \"unlock\", \"leverage\", \"seamless\", \"robust\", \"cutting-edge\", \"game-changer\". Em-dashes capped at ONE per paragraph. Sentence rhythm varied (long then short, fragments, occasional sentence starting with \"And\"/\"But\").\nDESIGN SYSTEM (HP white-paper enterprise-consumer): Space Grotesk display + Manrope body/UI. ONE signal color — HP Electric Blue #024ad8 — every CTA/link/focus ring. Near-black ink #1a1a1a headlines. White canvas, cloud #f7f7f7 cards, 16px radii on cards/photos, 4px on buttons/inputs. Dark navy (#1a1a1a) slabs for testimonials + closing CTA. Blue chevron decorations sparingly. Buttons: 14px Manrope 600 uppercase +0.7px tracking, 4px radius — non-negotiable. NO gradients. NO drop shadows on text. NO warm accents. NO rounded-full except avatars/pill tabs.\nANTI-REFERENCES (explicitly NOT this): generic AI-consultant sites (stock photos, buzzword copy, hero metric cards \"+300%\", gradient text, glassmorphism); course-platform look (Teachable/Kajabi sticky CTAs, \"join 10,000 students\", fake testimonials); SaaS dashboard aesthetic (feature grids, logo walls); AI slop (AI color palette, gradient text, Inter everywhere, bounce easing, three-tier pricing with middle highlighted, decorative blobs).\n\n\nDo a rigorous UX/UX-copy critique. Read the actual source — do not guess. Then produce a structured report with EXACTLY these sections:\n\n1. DESIGN-SPECIFICITY VERDICT: Is the composition, interaction, and visual language grounded in THIS product (last-AI-consultant, exit-as-goal, HP white-paper), or could an unrelated product use it unchanged? One paragraph + a verdict (Grounded / Partially / Generic).\n\n2. NIELSEN HEURISTICS (score each 0-4; 4=exemplary, 0=catastrophic; mark n/a only if truly inapplicable to a marketing surface). For each give the score and a SPECIFIC finding with file reference:\n  1 Visibility of System Status\n  2 Match System / Real World\n  3 User Control and Freedom\n  4 Consistency and Standards\n  5 Error Prevention\n  6 Recognition Rather Than Recall\n  7 Flexibility and Efficiency\n  8 Aesthetic and Minimalist Design\n  9 Error Recovery\n  10 Help and Documentation\n\n3. COGNITIVE LOAD: Report (a) any decision point where >4 options are visible at once, (b) any 5-second first-action clarity failures, (c) icon-only elements without text labels, (d) jargon assuming prior knowledge. Quote file:line.\n\n4. EMOTIONAL JOURNEY (peak-end): Walk the section order top to bottom. Where are the peaks, where are the valleys, where does the page reassure at high-stakes moments (email capture, pricing, \"am I being sold to\")? Does the \"exit as goal\" promise survive the funnel or get undercut by a hard sell?\n\n5. COPY AUDIT (this is half the ask — be thorough): Scan ALL section copy and the assessment flow. Report:\n   - Anti-slop violations: each instance of a kill-list word/phrase with file:line and the offending text.\n   - Em-dash density: any paragraph with >1 em-dash (file:line).\n   - Voice breaks: copy that drifts from direct/warm/expert into hype, corporate, or course-platform register (file:line + quote).\n   - Unverifiable claims: any stat or boast presented without a way to verify it (file:line + quote) — this directly violates the brand's \"density of fact\" principle.\n   - Missing \"exit\" framing: CTAs/sections that sell retention instead of independence.\n   - Clarity: jargon, vague abstractions, sentences a time-poor skeptic would skip.\n   Give a copy-health verdict (Strong / Needs Work / Slop Detected) with the top copy issues ranked.\n\n6. STRENGTHS (2-3, specific).\n\n7. PRIORITY ISSUES (3-5, ranked, each with file:line and a one-line fix direction).\n\n8. PERSONA RED FLAGS — test against: Jordan (first-impression skeptic), Riley (stress tester / edge cases on assessment form + ROI calc), Casey (one-handed mobile, thumb zone, interrupted), PLUS a project persona \"Morgan\" = established consultant who already pays for AI tools and has been burned by an AI workshop — what specifically would make Morgan bounce? List red flags per persona.\n\n9. MINOR OBSERVATIONS (bulleted).\n\n10. PROVOCATIVE QUESTIONS (2-3 a design director would ask the founder).\n\nBe specific and evidence-based. Quote real text and real classNames with file:line. No hedging. Return the full structured report as your message.",
